==English==
{{wp}}

===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|la|petītiō prīncipiī|lit=an assumption from the beginning}}, {{glink|calque}} of {{uder|en|grc|τὸ ἐν ἀρχῇ αἰτεῖσθαι||to assume from the beginning}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/pɪˈtɪʃiˌəʊ prɪnˈkɪpiˌaɪ/|a=RP}}
* {{a|en|GA}} {{enPR|pə-tĭshʹē-ō' prĭn-sĭpʹē-ī'}}, {{IPA|en|/pəˈtɪʃiˌoʊ prɪnˈsɪpiˌaɪ/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|?|nolinkhead=1}}

# {{lb|en|philosophy|logic|uncountable}} The logical [[fallacy]] of [[begging the question]] (i.e., "[[assume|assuming]] the [[conclusion]]").
# {{lb|en|philosophy|logic|countable}} A particular [[argument]] which commits the fallacy of begging the question; a [[circular argument]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1869|author=C. S. Pierce|chapter=Grounds of Validity of the laws of Logic: Further Consequences of Four Incapacities.|title=Journal of Speculative Philosophy
|passage=A somewhat similar objection has been made by Locke and others, to the effect that the ordinary demonstrative syllogism is a '''petitio principii'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1938|author=E. Prokosch|title=A Comparative Germanic Grammar
|passage=The Streitberg-Michels Theory is evidently a '''petitio principii'''. To explain ''ē'' in ''gēbum'', it is from the outset taken for granted, for inadequate reasons of method, that the form ''must'' be a perfect. [italics original]}}

